    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d402">

                <pb facs="0484" n="380" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg380"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/4289: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai (Cunningham) to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-18T15:00:00+08:00">February 18, 1932—3 [<hi
                                rendition="#italic">7</hi>] p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 3:43
                        p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>73. Consular body at meeting February 17, decided to send letters in following
                    sense <hi rendition="#italic">mutatis mutandis</hi> to Japanese Consul General
                    and Mayor of Shanghai municipality.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“In view of the immense importance of the port of Shanghai in world commerce
                        I have the honor to state that it is the earnest desire <pb facs="0485"
                            n="381" xml:id="pg381"/> of my interested colleagues and myself to take
                        all steps possible to maintain the trade and shipping of our respective
                        countries during the present critical time. With this end in view we trust
                        that the responsible Japanese authorities will without qualification
                        undertake not to interfere either now or in the future with the [trade?] and
                        shipping of the various countries of Europe and America at this port and
                        that you will see fit to make a declaration in this sense.</p>
                    <p>A similar communication is being addressed to the Chinese authorities
                        concerned and the favor of a prompt reply is requested.”</p></quote>
                <p>Letters will probably be sent this evening.</p>
                <p>In proposing foregoing action to consular body I had in mind compliance with
                    paragraph three B [<hi rendition="#italic">sic</hi>] of Department’s February 6,
                    8 p.m.<anchor n="61" xml:id="d402-f1"/></p>
                <p>Repeated to the Legation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Cunningham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
